# Indispensable Labs apps repository -- repo-info template.
#
# Substituted by packaging/build-repo.sh and published as `repo.info` at the
# repository base URL. This is the file `pkgman add-repo <URL>` fetches first
# (AddRepositoryRequest.cpp:53 appends "/repo.info"); it is also the input to
# `package_repo create`.
#
# FORMAT: driver_settings text, NOT a flattened BMessage. Parsed by
# BRepositoryInfo::_SetTo(const BEntry&), which reads the key `baseurl` -- all
# lowercase. The BMessage field constant is spelled `baseUrl`, a different code
# path that does not apply here.
#
# Required or the file is rejected as B_BAD_DATA: name, identifier (or the
# legacy `url`), vendor, summary, priority, architecture.
#
# This repository is deliberately SEPARATE from the Media-OS one. Media-OS ships
# kernel drivers; this ships ordinary userland apps. They share the `hpkg`
# bucket and the hpkg.indispensablelabs.com domain, but nothing else -- a
# different key prefix, a different identifier, a different name.

# ⚠️ MUST BE QUOTED. This is driver_settings format, where an unquoted value
# ends at the first whitespace -- an unquoted `Indispensable Labs` parses as the
# name "Indispensable" with a stray second argument, and `package_repo list`
# will show it truncated. Media-OS never hit this because "Media-OS" is a single
# token. Caught here on the first build, 2026-08-29, before publishing.
#
# The name is also the key Haiku matches an existing repository config by
# (ActivateRepositoryConfigJob.cpp:41), so getting it wrong is not cosmetic --
# it is what users pass to `pkgman drop-repo`.
name			"Indispensable Labs"

# ⚠️ MUST MATCH THE `vendor` FIELD OF EVERY PACKAGE IN THE REPOSITORY.
#
# package_repo refuses to build otherwise: RepositoryWriterImpl.cpp:405-410
# compares the two (case-insensitively) and errors with "package '<name>' has
# unexpected vendor". This bit the Media-OS repository on its first real build.
#
# Note this is the PACKAGER's vendor, not the software's author. Beton is
# Daniel Weber's MIT-licensed work (see its copyrights field); "Indispensable
# Labs" is who built and published this particular .hpkg.
vendor			"Indispensable Labs"

# THE ONE PIECE OF TEXT EVERY USER SEES BEFORE INSTALLING.
summary			"Native Haiku applications packaged by Indispensable Labs. Ordinary userland apps only -- this repository contains no drivers and touches nothing outside the package system. For the Media-OS driver work, see the separate media-os repository."

# STABLE IDENTITY. NEVER CHANGE IT for a given architecture. RFC 4151 tag: URI;
# the year is when the domain was held, not the release date.
#
# ⚠️ Do NOT assume the identifier lets the repository move hosts for free. It
# does not. Haiku selects an existing repository config by repository NAME:
#
#   ActivateRepositoryConfigJob.cpp:41  fTargetEntry.SetTo(&fTargetDirectory,
#                                           repoInfo.Name().String())
#
# The identifier is only written into the config (:70) and printed by
# `pkgman list-repos`; no code path matches on it. A URL change therefore costs
# every existing subscriber a manual step, and `add-repo <newURL>` alone prompts
# "already exists, overwrite?" with a default of NO -- so a user pressing enter
# silently stays on the dead URL. Tell people to `drop-repo "Indispensable Labs"`
# first; that form takes the NAME and has no wrong default.
#
# NO HAIKU RELEASE IN THE IDENTIFIER OR THE URL. Compatibility is declared per
# package with `requires { haiku >= ... }`, which is where Haiku actually
# enforces it; nothing in the package kit parses the base URL
# (RepositoryConfig.cpp:265 only ever appends "/packages" to it).
identifier		tag:indispensablelabs.com,2026:repositories/apps/@ARCH@

baseurl			@BASEURL@

# Lower number = higher precedence: LibsolvSolver.cpp:748 sets
# `repo->priority = -1 - Priority()`, and libsolv prefers the larger value.
# Haiku and HaikuPorts both publish 1. 50 is BRepositoryInfo's default and the
# correct choice for a third party: it must never outrank the official
# repositories on a name collision.
priority		50

# Must be a concrete architecture -- `any` is explicitly rejected
# (RepositoryInfo.cpp:395-397). Arch-independent packages still live in an
# arch-specific repository; that is how HaikuPorts does it too.
architecture	@ARCH@
